<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <SupportedOSPlatform>windows</SupportedOSPlatform>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!-- See LiveClr.csproj: enforced doc coverage is deferred until the API surface settles. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Reflection/ (the getter-code offset decoder) needs a complete x86-64 decoder. docs/analysis.md
    §8.1's rule is "borrow for metadata, hand-roll the rest". The hand-rolled side of that line is
    for formats that are ~40 lines (PE headers, the descriptor). x86-64 instruction encoding is not
    one of those: a partial decoder mis-decodes silently, which is precisely the failure mode
    §8.9/AbsentNeverWrongTests exist to prevent. Iced is MIT, has no transitive dependencies, and is
    the reference-quality decoder for .NET.

    CONFINED ON PURPOSE. Nothing outside src/Godot.External/Reflection/ may reference Iced.Intel, so
    if the core library must return to a BCL-only dependency graph before packaging (§8.6), that
    folder lifts out into its own project without touching anything else.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Iced" Version="1.21.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    NOT PACKABLE YET. Per docs/analysis.md §8.6/§8.9 this stays private until the ABI grid
    (tools/godot-abi-grid) shows the calibrator solving layouts it has never seen. Shipping the
    §4.6 offset table without that evidence would publish one validated cell of a large matrix
    as if it were general.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The memory-access surface (IByteSource and the Values/ decoders) is deliberately internal: it
    is a two-method seam that will be re-pointed at LiveClr's IMemoryReader (§8.8). Tests drive it
    with synthetic byte arrays rather than the seam being widened into public API.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Godot.External.Tests" />
    <!--
      The calibrator (docs/analysis.md §12.5) is the one production consumer that needs the seam
      itself rather than the library above it: it supplies the IByteSource adapter over LiveClr's
      reader, and it drives the CowData/StringName decoders against CANDIDATE offsets, before any
      profile exists to read them through. Widening the seam into public API to serve it would
      export the very thing §8.8 keeps replaceable.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Godot.External.Calibrator" />
    <!--
      bench/ measures the read path from outside the library but below its API: it needs IByteSource
      to install a counting decorator under the cache, and MemorySnapshot to install the cache at
      all. Measuring only through the public surface would leave the syscall count — the number the
      whole exercise is about — unobservable.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Godot.External.Bench" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
